    <exercise>
        <title>Energy of two particles in the CM frame</title>
        <p>
            Show that the kinetic energy of a two-particle system, 
            <m>K=\frac{1}{2} m_1 v_1^2+\frac{1}{2} m_2 v_2^2 = \frac{1}{2} MV_{CM}^2+\frac{1/2}μV_r^2</m>, where <m>V_{CM}</m> is the speed of the 
            center of mass; <m>V_r</m> is the relative speed between the two particles, and 
            <m>\mu=\dfrac{m_1 m_2}{m_1+m_2}</m>. Hint: start by solving for <m>(v_1,v_2)</m> in terms of 
            <m>(V_{CM},V_r)</m>. You may work in one dimension instead of using vectors, but this result is valid in 3D.
        </p>
        
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
        <title>Conservation of Energy-Like Problems in Other Contexts</title>
        <introduction>
        <p>
            Whenever you see a differential equation of the type <m>~{\dfrac{d^2 x}{dt^2}=-\dfrac{dV}{dx}}</m>, 
            even if mechanics doesn't have anything to do with it, you can apply <q>conservation of energy</q> to 
            say that <m>\frac{1}{2}\dot{x}^2+V(x)=E</m> is constant and use energy-diagram methods to learn about 
            your solution!
        </p> 
        <p> 
            One such example is the <term>Friedmann equation</term>, which describes the expansion of the universe
            using a scale factor <m>a(t)</m> that describes how length scales increase with time. If <m>a(t)</m>
            changes from 1 to 2, for instance, this means that the distance between two objects will double in the
            time it takes for that to happen. 
        </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                     The Friedmann equation for a universe with just matter and dark energy can be written, after some
                     simplification, as <md>\dfrac{d^2 a}{dt^2}=-\dfrac{B}{a^2} +2Wa</md>.
                     <m>B,W</m> are positive constants, related, respectively, to the amount of matter and dark energy in the 
                     universe. Find the <q>potential energy</q> <m>V(a)</m> for this problem
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Sketch a graph of the potential energy. Note: the particular values of <m>B,W</m> aren't 
                     super important here, all that matters is the general shape of the graph.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <q>total energy</q> <m>E</m> in the Friedmann equation can be positive, negative, or zero. 
                    Assuming that the universe starts from a small size (so <m>a\simeq 0</m> at some point in time),
                    use your energy diagram to describe the possible fates of the universe, and take some time to ponder that 
                    it is possible to do so much with very little math (once you have the Friedmann equation!).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>